-- Czech Republic story arc: only 2 stories, deliberately, matching
-- Cyprus's own thin-B2B-picture precedent -- but built around the
-- one genuinely live development this country has that Cyprus never
-- did: EET 2.0's passage through the Chamber of Deputies.

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO stories (id, date, month, summary_en, html_en, source_url, published) VALUES ('2026-07-17-czech-eet2-passes-lower-house', '2026-07-17', '2026-07', 'The Chamber of Deputies has passed EET 2.0, a revived real-time electronic sales-reporting regime effective 1 January 2027 -- a B2C point-of-sale duty, not a B2B e-invoicing mandate.', '<h3>🇨🇿 Czech Republic''s lower house passes a sales-reporting revival -- but this isn''t an invoicing mandate</h3>
<p>The Czech Republic''s actual e-invoicing law remains narrow: since 1 October 2016, public contracting authorities have had to accept EN 16931-compliant e-invoices from suppliers, via the Národní elektronický nástroj (NEN) portal. That''s genuinely all that''s legally required today for invoicing -- nothing obliges a supplier to actually send one, and there is no B2B mandate of any kind, nor is one currently proposed.</p>
<p>What is moving, and moving fast, is something different: a revived Electronic Registration of Sales regime, EET 2.0, which the Chamber of Deputies has now passed. It will require real-time or near-real-time reporting of in-person B2C sales -- cash, card, and QR-code payments alike -- to the Tax Administration from 1 January 2027, replacing the original EET model that ran roughly 2016-2020 before formal repeal. Small flat-rate-tax entrepreneurs under CZK 1,000,000 annual revenue can pay a surcharge for exemption, and the government projects CZK 14-15 billion in additional annual tax revenue. Non-compliance carries penalties of up to CZK 500,000.</p>
<p>The bill now moves to the Senate, then to President Petr Pavel for final signature -- both still pending. And it''s worth being precise about the scope: EET 2.0 is a sales/receipt-reporting duty at the point of sale, not a structured B2B e-invoicing mandate. No invoice format is specified, and there''s no buyer-seller document exchange requirement -- the same shape as Spain''s VeriFactu, not South Korea''s or Turkey''s invoice-reporting systems.</p>', 'https://www.expats.cz/czech-news/article/czechia-approves-return-of-eet-electronic-sales-tracking-to-resume-in-2027', 1);
